and ${\sf D}_{2}$ within confined narrow pore system. Such kinetic separation is achieved through different diffusion barriers from the difference in the zero-point energy of the adsorbed $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ isotopes. Kinetic quantum molecular sieving may be observed for $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorption when the difference between the molecular diameter and the pore diameter of adsorbents becomes comparable to the de Broglie wavelength, which usually happens in pore structure of about $0.6\mathrm{nm}$ . For example, Kaneko et al. reported that [Cu $(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}(\mathsf{C F}_{3}\mathsf{S O}_{3})_{2}]$ with two different pores of 8.7 and $2.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ can separate $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixture at $77\mathrm{K}$ with a selectivity of 1.2 [95]. Also, open metal sites in MOFs can enlarge the binding difference of $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ with the framework, thus realize their separation. For example, FitzGerald et al. reported that the ${\sf D}_{2}$ adsorption heat in Ni-MOF74 is larger than that for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with a difference of $1.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , resulting a zero-pressure selectivity of 5 at 77 K [96]. Simultaneously, incorporating open metal centers into ultramicropore MOFs for binding hydrogen molecules under the confinement of pore space not only can enhance the hydrogen binding affinity but also maximize the quantum effects, affording higher performance of kinetic $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation. As a rational strategy that can systematically immobilize different open metal sites within porous MOFs, metalloligand approach is very promising on this endeavor. The first experimental results for $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation using MOFs were published in 2008 [93]. We developed a microporous mixedmetal–organic framework (M0MOF) $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}(\mathrm{CuPyen})]$ (M0MOF1, $\mathrm{PyenH}_{2}=5$ -methyl-4-oxo-1,4-dihydro-pyridine-3-carbalde hyde) with enhanced affinity for hydrogen molecules, which successfully separated ${\sf D}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through kinetic quantum molecular sieving. M0MOF-1 is composed of trinuclear $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{3}(\mathsf{R C O O})_{6}$ clusters bridged by $\mathsf{b d c}^{2-}$ and preconstructed metalloligand ${\mathrm{Cu}}({\mathrm{Pyen}})$ to form three dimensional framework with two types of micropores $(\leq5.6\mathring\mathrm{A},$ Fig. 4). The immobilized $\mathtt{C u}$ centers with two open sites on the pore surface are accessible for gas molecules after activation. Adsorption measurements for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and ${\sf D}_{2}$ revealed comparable molar $\mathsf{D}_{2}/\mathsf{H}_{2}$ ratios $(n\mathsf{D}_{2}/n\mathsf{H}_{2})$ of $1.09{-}1.11\$ attributed to quantum effect. The isosteric enthalpies of adsorption $(Q_{s\mathrm{t,n=0}})$ at zero surface coverage were calculated to be 12.29(53) and $12.44(50)\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and ${\sf D}_{2}$ , respectively. Adsorption kinetics analyses for the diffusion of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and ${\sf D}_{2}$ during adsorption in M0MOF-1 revealed that the heavier ${\sf D}_{2}$ were faster than $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , showing average ratios of rate constants $k\mathsf{D}_{2}/k\mathsf{H}_{2}$ of 1.62 and 1.38 for two components ( $\cdot_{k_{1}}$ and $k_{2}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ ), respectively, corresponding to diffusion along two types of pores. The ${\sf D}_{2}$ kinetic barriers (activation energy) related to its zeropoint energy were determined to be 12.52 and $8.04\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for both components, which are slightly lower than the corresponding values for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ kinetics for both components. Such difference of adsorption kinetics was attributed to quantum effects that the higher effective collision cross section of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ related to its higher zero point energy can result in a higher barrier for diffusion along the pores as compared to ${\sf D}_{2}$ , which might be utilized for the kinetic sieving $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation. This is the first experimental observation of porous MOF materials for kinetic isotope quantum molecular sieving.
Notably, by virtue of metallo-ligand approach, the above endeavour firstly realized porous MOFs for the challenging kinetic sieving $\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation. After this pioneering work, extensive research interests have been attracted to explore MOFs on kinetic isotope quantum molecular sieving [62,97], affording further improved performance for this very important application.
## 2.3. Separations of $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}$
Ethylene $\mathrm{\cdot}_{\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ , the most produced organic compound in the world, over 150 million tons/year in 2016) is essential raw chemical widely used in the manufacture of many polymer products and useful chemicals. Ethylene is produced in the petrochemical industry by steam cracking, accompanied by some other byproducts and/or impurities. Among them, acetylene is one important byproduct/impurity of approximately $1\%$ concentration, because it can seriously affect the polymerization of ethylene by poisoning the catalyst during the production of polyethylene, a most important and widely used plastic. Also, acetylene is explosive above $200\mathrm{kPa}$ (29 psi), and can form solid metal acetylides that would block the cracked gas stream, resulting in huge safety risk. Therefore, during the ethylene production, acetylene must be reduced to an acceptable level. To meet the concentration limit of below 40 ppm (parts per million), cryogenic distillation or partial hydrogenation are applied to remove or transform acetylene, which is energy intensive. In contrast, adsorptive separation using MOFs for this challenging acetylene removal provide a more efficient way, avoiding the use of noble metals and solvents, which has attracted great interest. Also, adsorptive acetylene capture can produce pure acetylene gas to better utilize and meet the industrial need [98]. In fact, the adsorptive separation performance of MOFs for the acetylene/ethylene mixture has been extensively examined, but only a few of them show some promise. This is because acetylene and ethylene show similar molecular sizes, polarizabilities, dipole moments and other physical/chemical properties.
Xiang et al. reported the first example of microporous MOFs for this challenging acetylene/ethylene separation in 2011 [99]. Isostructural mixed-metal–organic frameworks (M0MOFs) have been developed from metalloligand $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{S a l P y c y})$ by systematically tuning their micropores for optimized $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities. Two M0MOFs $\mathrm{Zn_{3}(b d c)_{3}[C u(S a l P y c y)]}$ (M0MOF-2) and $Z n_{3}(\mathrm{cdc})_{3}[\mathrm{Cu}$ (SalPycy)] (M0MOF-3, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{cdc}}=1,4$ -cyclohexanedicarboxylate acid) (Fig. 1) were readily assembled by solvothermal reactions of predesigned metalloligand Cu(SalPyCy) with $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cdc}$ (Fig. 5), exhibiting chiral pore cavities with immobilization of open Cu centers. M0MOF-2 and M0MOF-3 are isostructural three dimensional frameworks, in which trinuclear $Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{RCOO})_{6}$ clusters are connected by $\mathsf{b d c}^{2-}$ or $\operatorname{cdc}^{2-}$ ligands to form two dimensional $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}$ or $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{3}(\mathrm{cdc})_{3}$ layers that are further pillared by the chiral metalloligand $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{S a l P y C y})$ . Due to the more flexible $\mathsf{c d c}^{2-}$ ligand, activated M0MOF-3a has smaller pores as compared to M0MOF-2a, which is confirmed by adsorption isotherms of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at 195 K. Thus, at 195 K, M0MOF-3a exhibited a significantly higher $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of 25.5, while M0MOF-2a can adsorb both ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ resulting in a low selectivity of 1.6. The higher selectivity of M0MOF-3a was attributed to enhanced sieving effects in M0MOF-3a. Compared with the molecular size of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ (kinetic diameters: $4.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ), the smaller $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\left(3.3\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ allowed it to fulfill more efficient filling in the micropores of M0MOF-3a. In contrast, ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ molecules are basically blocked or the kinetics is very slow. Remarkably, the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of M0MOF-3a at $295\mathrm{K}$ was calculated to be 5.2, making this material a practically promising adsorbent for this important separation. Overall, the subtle tuning of the micropores in metalloligand-based MOFs resulted in significantly enhanced $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation.
The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities of these M0MOFs can be further optimized after systematically tuning their micropores by combining different metalloligands and organic ligands, as revealed by series isostructural M0MOFs [27]. These activated M0MOFs are promising for effective removal of trace ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ from $1/99\ C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm C_{2}\mathrm H_{4}$ mixture at room temperature. These M0MOFs can reduce the ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ concentration to below $40\mathrm{ppm}$ to meet the polymerization requirement, resulting in high purity ethylene. The potential application of these M0MOFs for the fixed-bed adsorptive separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ at ambient conditions has been further confirmed by transient breakthrough simulations. Among different M0MOFs, M0MOF-4a showed the best $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation performance. Obviously, the sieving effects from the ultra-micro pores of these M0MOFs resulted in high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities. However, the small pore volumes would simultaneously limit their acetylene adsorption capacity, which definitely affects their actual performance for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation. Further development was extended to the evaluation of MMOF-74 series with open metal sites for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation in 2012 [100]. Notably, Fe-MOF-74 reported by Long et al. can exhibit remarkable $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation with selectivities of 13–18 and 13–15, which is higher than classical adsorbents such as zeolite $_{\sf N a X}$ and other MOFs [22]. Such separation is attributed to the much stronger interactions of ethylene and propylene with the metal sites as confirmed by neutron powder diffraction experiments. Indeed, by virtue of high densities of open metal sites, these M-MOF-74 materials indeed showed high uptake capacity and strong binding affinity for unsaturated hydrocarbons including acetylene. However, the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities of M-MOF-74 are quite low because the open metal sites can also bind strongly with ethylene.
Ideal porous materials for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation should exhibit high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity and optimal $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at ambient conditions. Dual-functionalized MOFs with suitable pore/aperture size and accessible strong binding sites can make it a reality [102]. After our extensive research endeavours on microporous MOFs for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation [103–107], we recently realized a unique dual-functionalized MOF [Cu(atbdc)] (UTSA-100, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{atbdc}=5.$ -(5-amino-1H-tetrazol-1-yl)-1,3-benzene dicarboxylic acid) for efficient removal of acetylene from 1/99 $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture [101]. This MOF is composed of 6-connected paddle-wheel copper clusters bridged by 3-connected atbdc2 linkers to form three dimensional framework with apo topology. UTSA-100a contains one-dimensional channels of about $4.{\overset{-}{3}}{\overset{8}{\mathrm{A}}}$ and small cavities of $4.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ with apertures of $3.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Fig. 6), exhibiting considerable Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of 970 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Thanks to suitable pore sizes and immobilized $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ groups as binding sites, UTSA-100a took up considerable $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ of $95.6\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ but much lower ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ of $37.2\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\thinspace{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at ambient conditions, resulting in high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity (10.7) for $1/99\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/$ ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ mixture. The combination of high adsorption selectivity and high uptake capacity at ambient conditions allows UTSA-100a to remove trace ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ more efficiently (showing higher ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity from $1/99~\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture), compared with the above mentioned M0MOFs, M-MOF-74 series and NOTT-300. The superior performance of UTSA-100a in trace acetylene removal was confirmed by simulated and experimental breakthrough studies. Detailed pore structure analysis and first-principles DFT-D (dispersion-corrected density-functional theory) calculations revealed that suitable pore size and binding sites play roles for highly efficient $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation of UTSA-100a.
As demonstrated above, those MOF materials already have improved separation performance as compared to classical materials, but they still suffer from the trade-off of adsorption capacity versus selectivity for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation. This trade-off problem can be overcome through pore chemistry and size control. Very recently, Xing and Chen et al. reported a series of $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ -pillared MOF materials (SIFSIX) with tunable pore sizes, providing a unique platform for optimizing host–guest and/or guest–guest interactions, which exhibit exceptional ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ capture performance for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures [108]. In these pillar-layered SIFSIX materials,
