the organic ligand layers are pillared by $\mathsf{S i F}_{6}^{2-}$ anions on metal nodes, in which the square-grid sizes can be easily tuned by using various organic linkers of different lengths, giving various MOFs with tunable pore sizes. On the other hand, accessible $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ anions as hydrogen-bonding donors exposed on the pore surface provide multiple specific sites for acetylene molecules. After optimized investigation, it was found that SIFSIX-2-Cu-i $\cdot2=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -dipyridylace tylene, $\dot{\bf l}=$ interpenetrated) shows unprecedentedly high $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $2.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.025 bar and $298\mathrm{K},$ resulting in record $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities (39.7–44.8) estimated by IAST, which is the most suitable material for the ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ purification from $1/99~\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture (Fig. 7). And SIFSIX-1-Cu ( $1=4{,}4{^\prime}$ -bipyri dine) shows the highest ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ storage capacity of $8.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and captured capacity of $6.37\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ from $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ mixture at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ , which is most conducive to separate $50/50\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture. High-resolution neutron powder diffraction study of $\phantom{+}C_{2}\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -loaded SIFSIX-2-Cu-i sample showed that acetylene molecule interacts simultaneously with two fluorine atoms from different nets through cooperative C–H  F hydrogen bonds, and there are also van der Waals interactions between acetylene molecules and the organic linkers. For $\phantom{+}C_{2}\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -loaded SIFSIX-1-Cu sample, besides similar types of host–guest interaction as in SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, there are also guest–guest interactions $(\mathrm{H}^{\S+}...{\cal C}^{\S-}$ dipole–dipole interactions) in the cluster of four acetylene molecules. The distinct locations of molecules with different interactions in the pores of SIFSIX well explained the difference in terms of adsorption capacity and selectivity, which were further confirmed by detailed molecular modeling studies. Using first-principles DFT-D calculations, static adsorption energy for acetylene molecule (DE) is calculated to be 52.9 and $44.6\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ in SIFSIX-1-Cu and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, respectively. For ethylene molecules in these SIFSIX materials, their binding strengths are much weaker than acetylene, resulting in highly selective ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ recognition. Highly efficient separations for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures were realized in SIFSIX materials as demonstrated by breakthrough experiments and simulations for two $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures $(1/99$ and 50/50) that mimic the industrial process conditions. Indeed, trace acetylene impurity can be removed to less than 2 ppm, generating high-purity ethylene. Further breakthrough experiments conducted on the presence of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , moisture and/or $0_{2}$ demonstrated well the stability of SIFSIX materials during separation processes. And breakthrough cycling measurements also revealed their good regenerability during many test cycles. By virtue of pore size and chemistry control, these SIFSIX materials show distinct host–guest and guest–guest interactions for acetylene molecules, resulting in extremely high ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ capture performance, which afford new benchmarks for the highly efficient ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ purification and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ separation from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures under ambient conditions.
In general, only very few reported porous materials for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ can fulfill partial sieving effect but not belong to exclusive separation, which means certain co-adsorption of ethylene is unavoidable leading to the waste of partial ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ production capacity. The ideal $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation is that only acetylene molecules are adsorbed into porous adsorbents while ethylene molecules are completely blocked outside, generating complete sieving effect. Therefore, it is speculated that the ideal pore aperture size of porous MOFs should be always smaller than the molecular size of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ (kinetic diameter: $4.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\dot{}$ but larger than that of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ during adsorption processes. On the other hand, the porosity of the adsorbent should be as high as possible to maximize acetylene uptake for high separation productivity. In fact, it is a daunting challenge to design and synthesize such porous MOFs. Very recently, during our exploration on ultramicroporous MOF materials, we discovered a novel SIFSIX material UTSA-300 with pore aperture size of about $3.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and multiple potential binding sites, showing complete ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ exclusion from the mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ under ambient conditions [109]. UTSA-300 can only take up acetylene $(76.4\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 273 K), but no noticeable ethylene uptake can be observed even at close to its liquefaction temperature. Crystal structures, molecular modeling, selectivity calculation, and experimental breakthrough experiment comprehensively demonstrated this unique material for highly selective $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation.
## 2.4. Sulfur dioxide capture
The efficient capture of sulfur dioxide $(\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ is very important for gas-purification processes including flue-gas desulfurization and natural-gas purification, which can avoid direct detriment on environment and human health or poisoning catalyst. Moreover, trace $S0_{2}$ can also inactivate the adsorbents or absorbents for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ removal from flue gas. Therefore, there is a great demand for efficient technologies for trace $S0_{2}$ removal from flue gas and other $S0_{2}$ -containing gases to replace traditional energy-intensive desulfurization processes. Some early studies have demonstrated that the removal of $S0_{2}$ from flue gas can be realized by using many types of adsorbents, especially for high concentration $S0_{2}$ . For example, a tetramethylguanidine lactate ionic liquid can absorb $S0_{2}$ from $8/92~\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture with capture capacity of $_{0.305\mathrm{g}}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $40^{\circ}C$ and 1 bar, while its $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ absorption capacity is only of $0.25\mathrm{wt\%}$ [111]. Such chemical absorption is so strong that its desorption can be partially achieved only at very high temperature, as evidenced by the capture capacity of $0.160{\bf g}{\bf g}^{-1}$ at $94^{\circ}C$ , which requires high regeneration energy. Some prussian blues were demonstrated can also take up considerable $S0_{2}$ [112]. As revealed by their single component gas sorption isotherms, their adsorption capacity for $S0_{2}$ reach to $2.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity are up to $1.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, considering the trace amount $S0_{2}$ in flue gas (about $2000\mathrm{ppm},$ ), the low $S0_{2}/C0_{2}$ selectivity is unfavourable as the co-adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ might sacrifice the usage capacity for $S0_{2}$ . Thus, high $S0_{2}/C0_{2}$ selectivity is essential for adsorbent materials. In fact, MOFs have been demonstrated as efficient adsorbents to address the challenging $S0_{2}$ removal, which can capture $S0_{2}$ at very low concentration with very high selectivity. Very recently, Chen and Xing et al. comprehensively evaluated hexafluorosilicate (SIFSIX) based MOFs for highly selective recognition of $S0_{2}$ from gas mixtures, demonstrating MOFs as superior porous adsorbent for highly efficient removal of trace $S0_{2}$ (Fig. 8) [110]. By virtue of pore size tuning $(4{-}10\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ and multiple binding sites functionalization, SIFSIX materials can exhibit remarkable performance for different gases separation. Typically, SIFSIX materials exhibit a pillared square-grid 3D structure containing 1D pore channels functionalized with hexafluorosilicate F sites. As revealed by X-ray crystal structures and molecular modeling studies, the adsorbed $S0_{2}$ molecules interacted with the host frameworks through multiple synergistic host–guest and guest–guest interactions, resulting in stronger binding affinity (highest isosteric adsorption heats of $36{-}45\mathrm{kJ/mol};$ . In SIFSIX materials, $S0_{2}$ interacts simultaneously with fluorine atoms and pyridine linker through cooperative $\hat{S}^{\delta^{+}}{\cdot\cdot\cdot\hat{\mathrm{F}}^{\delta-}}$ electrostatic interactions and $0^{\delta-}.$ - $\therefore\cdot\cdot\mathrm{H}^{\delta+}$ dipole–dipole interactions, accompanied by guest–guest interactions between $S0_{2}$ molecules. These strong interactions resulted in highly efficient removal of $S0_{2}$ from other gases, even if at a very low $S0_{2}$ concentration. At 298 K and 1 bar, SIFSIX-1- Cu showed exceptional and recorded $S0_{2}$ uptake capacity of $11.01\mathrm{\mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . All investigated SIFSIX materials showed remarkable $S0_{2}$ uptake capacity even at very low pressure, being consistent with their strong binding affinity to $S0_{2}$ molecule as revealed by structural analyses. For example, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i showed unprecedented $S0_{2}$ capture capacity of $2.31\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $S0_{2}$ partial pressure of 0.002 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ under flowed $S O_{2}/N_{2}$ mixed gas, which is equivalent to the concentration of $S0_{2}$ in flue gas $(2000\mathrm{ppm})$ . Thus, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i has great potential in flue-gas desulfurization applications. Importantly, these SIFSIX materials showed higher binding affinity and uptake capacity to $S0_{2}$ comparing with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , giving recorded $S0_{2}/C0_{2}$ selectivity $86-$ 89) and excellent $S O_{2}/N_{2}$ selectivity (1285–3145), which can ben
