The excellent performance reported in this study sets a new benchmark in the highly efficient removal of trace $S0_{2}$ from flue gas or natural gas using porous materials, which reveals a path forward for industrial related separations.
## 2.5. Carbon dioxide capture
The environmental and energy concern of carbon dioxide emission (as a major greenhouse gas) has drawn unprecedented attention to develop applicable and efficient technologies for carbon dioxide capture and separation. To capture carbon dioxide from flue gas, aqueous amine-based absorbents are applied on large scale to chemically adsorb it during practical process. However, the regeneration of these chemisorbents is usually carried out at high temperatures due to their extremely high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ affinity and poor reversibility, which is highly energy-intensive. Also, this technology can cause the corrosion of equipment and pipelines. In contrast, reversible ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorption using physiadsorbents such as MOFs is more energy-efficient. And, the exceptional uptake capacities of MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ storage at room temperature demonstrate that they can serve as good candidates during related process [113]. Compared with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , the other components of different gas mixtures (natural gas, flue gas etc.) like $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ show different physical properties (polarizability $\times10^{25}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ : ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ 29.1, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}~25.9$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ 17.4; quadrupole moment  $\mathrm{\Omega}\cdot10^{40}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ : ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ 13.4, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ 0 and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ 4.7). In general, the higher polarizability and quadrupole moment of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ result in stronger interactions with the pore surface of
MOFs, which can be further enhanced by incorporating polar functional sites, showing great potential in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture and separation [68,69]. In fact, most MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ separation belong to this type of thermodynamic separation. For those MOFs without explicit binding site, the relatively similar kinetic diameters of these molecules $\mathrm{~\CO}_{2}3.30$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}3.76\$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}3.64\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ) require the precisely tuning of their pore size for molecular sieving, which is a highly challenging task.
As mentioned earlier, interpenetration strategies are powerful to easily obtain MOFs with tunable pore sizes, which can realize selective recognition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ . The evaluation of ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ separation did not involve breakthrough fixed-bed experiment until in 2008 [114]. Bastin et al. demonstrated that the double interpenetrated $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]$ , described above with suitable pore size of about $4.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , can fulfill the capture and separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from it binary and ternary mixtures (Fig. 9). The pore size of MOF-508 is similar to the molecular sizes of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ (3.30, 3.76 and $3.64\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , respectively), but still allow diffusion of these three gas molecules to its confined space with considerable host–guest interaction. Single-component adsorption isotherms revealed that the adsorption capacities of MOF-508b for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ are 26.0, 5.5 and $3.2\mathrm{wt\%}$ respectively, at 4.5 bar and 303 K. The adsorption enthalpy at zero coverage for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is determined to be $14.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , which is much higher than those for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ . The potential of the MOF for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture and separation were checked first by single-component breakthrough experiments, resulting in a breakthrough time hierarchy of $\ C0_{2}>$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}>\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . All these results clearly confirm that ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is highly preferentially adsorbed by MOF-508b over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ . Further breakthrough fixed-bed experiments indicated that ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ can be efficiently removed from its binary $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${C O}_{2}/{\Nu_{2}}$ and ternary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm N_{2}/\mathrm C0_{2}$ mixtures, giving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity of 3 and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\big/}$
