${\sf N}_{2}$ selectivity of 6 as estimated from breakthrough experiments. This is the first example of porous MOFs for the real separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ in their binary and ternary mixtures, using fixed-bed breakthrough to mimic practical separation process. Since then, this challenging technology has gradually become a metrics to evaluate MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ separation.
Later, extensive research endeavours on functionalized MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ separation demonstrated that open metal sites (OMSs) [116–121], specific polar groups $(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , $-\mathsf{O H}$ , etc.) [122–129] or pore size controlling are efficient in improving ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ separation performance [130–138]. Ideally, immobilization of specific functional sites in MOFs can afford preferential binding sites for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , and simultaneously optimizing the pore/cage sizes can maximize the van der Waals interactions between the pore surfaces and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Such dual-functionality collaboratively results in high separation selectivity and capacity, realizing optimal ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture applications at ambient conditions [102]. During our exploration ultramicroporous MOF materials, we targeted a very special MOF $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathsf{K}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}.}\end{array}$ - $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{cit)(Hcit)}]$ (UTSA-16) from a very cheap chemical citric acid (Hcit) to exhibit remarkable performance for carbon capture [115]. UTSA-16 is a three-dimensional framework with dia topology, which is composed of $\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ clusters and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -polyhedra linkers via face-sharing and further infinite three-dimensional heteronuclear M-O-M connections. UTSA-16 contains three-dimensional channels of about $3.3\times5.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ and small cavities of $4.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Fig. 10), exhibiting considerable BET surface area of $628\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The activated UTSA-16 exhibited high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake of $160{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ , high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ selectivity (29.8) for $50/50~\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture and ${C O}_{2}/{\Nu}_{2}$ selectivity (314.7) for $15/85\ C0_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ mixture at ambient conditions, which are higher than those of most MOFs used for comparison. Simulated breakthrough experiments to evaluate the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture performance in fixed-bed adsorber further confirm that UTSA-16 exhibits high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and selectivity for binary $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${C O}_{2}/{\Nu}_{2}$ gas mixtures. Powder neutron diffraction studies demonstrated that remarkable performance of UTSA-16 for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture can be attributed to its optimal pore size and suitable binging sites for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (bind on the terminal coordinated water molecules through hydrogen bonding interactions). Such air-stable UTSA-16 synthesized from cheap raw materials enables it a potential candidate for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture and removal.
Hereafter, research endeavours from different researchers realized benchmarks in the highly efficient capture of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ using porous materials, facilitating practical application of this type of adsorbents [139]. To evaluate different solid adsorbents for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture under realistic conditions, Long et al. and Zaworotko et al. carried out equilibrium sorption experiments of gas mixtures including ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , respectively, using some representative MOFs, mesoporous silicas, zeolites and activated carbons that have received great attention for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture [140,141]. The aminemodified chemisorbents can keep their exceptional performance moist ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ stream although their adsorption heats are unfavourable for regeneration. Another approach is using water stable ultramicroporous MOFs. For example, Eddaoudi et al. demonstrated that NbOFFIVE-1-Ni can maintain its $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of about $2.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 6 months immersion in water [142]. Under humid condition $74\%$ RH), the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture capacity of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni for $1/99\ C0_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ mixture slightly decrease from $8.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ to $5.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ . By virtue of versatile functionalization strategies, the pore surface and size can be simultaneously controlled. Taking related factors during ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture into account (selectivity, adsorption capacity and kinetics, regeneration conditions, material stability, impurity tolerance, economic cost. . .) [63], designed MOFs can be competent for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ separation over a wide concentration range, being targeted to apply in flue gas treatment, natural gas processing, trace ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ removal in confined spaces, and air capture.
## 2.6. Separations of $C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}$
Acetylene $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ is an important source of many chemical products including acrylic acid derivatives, vinyl compounds, and $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ - ethynyl alcohols in the chemical industry since its can undergo a variety of addition reactions. Accordingly, the production of high purity acetylene is of great industrial significance. The big challenge is the coexisting of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ impurity during the production of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ , while ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ have almost identical sizes, shapes (3.3 $2\times3.34\times5.70$ and $3.18\times3.33\times5.36\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ , respectively), and physical properties (boiling points of 189.3 and $194.7\mathrm{K},$ respectively). Therefore, the exploitation of efficient physical $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbent is highly demanded. In general, immobilized functional sites in porous adsorbents can maximize the tiny difference between $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , leading specific recognition of these similar gas molecules. Open metal sites and electronegative Lewis base sites are demonstrated to be effective for enhancing the ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ binding affinity. Structurally, for electronegative Lewis base sites, ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ can bind to it as H-donor through hydrogen-bonding interaction, while ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ only interacts with its electropositive carbon through weaker electrostatic interaction. For open metal sites, ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ binds to it with pi system through stronger coordination bond, while ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ coordinates to metal site using its electronegative O atoms. The first example using MOF for selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ over ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was realized by Kitagawa et al. in 2005 [143]. They synthesized an ultramicroporous material with one-dimensional channels $(4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\times6\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ that can accommodate ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ well via strong hydrogen-bonding interactions, showing considerable $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation. Later, by virtue of unique kinetically controlled flexibility, Zhang and Chen reported a dynamic porous material for high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation performance, in which unprecedented ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ hexamer was found binding to electronegative N atoms via hydrogen-bonding interactions [144]. During the last several years, $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separations have been realized for a few more MOFs [145–152], and even for the first time employing a porous hydrogen-bonded organic framework HOF-3 to address such a separation [153].
Particularly, Luo, Chen and co-authors reported a novel MOF-74 isomer $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(d o b d c)(H_{2}O)]}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (Zn-UTSA-74, $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{d}\mathsf{o b d c}=2,5$ -dio xido-1,4-benzenedicar-boxylic acid) with two accessible binding sites per metal center for high $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ separation. Zn-UTSA-74 has novel four connected fgl topology with one-dimensional open channels of about $8.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ [154]. Unlike open metal sites in the well established MOF-74 that each metal center is in a five coordinate square pyramidal coordination geometry with only one open side, there are two different Zn centers within the binuclear secondary building units in Zn-UTSA-74, in which one Zn is in a tetrahedral while the other one in octahedral coordination geometry. After activation, the two axial water molecules on six-coordinated zinc centers can be removed, generating Zn-UTSA-74a with two accessible binding sites per Zn ion for gas molecules (Fig. 11). With the same open metal sites, Zn-UTSA-74a took up a moderately high and comparable amount of acetylene $145\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ ) to Zn-MOF74. X-ray crystal structures and molecular modeling studies revealed that open zinc sites bind with two $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per metal site and one molecule per metal site for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , respectively. Interestingly, the accessible open zinc sites in Zn-UTSA-74a are bridged by $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules rather than taking terminal-binding mode in Zn-MOF-74, so Zn-UTSA-74a adsorbs a much smaller amount of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $90\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ ) than Zn-MOF-74 $146\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ ) at
